# Git

**Do not commit, stage-for-commit, amend, push, or create PRs unless the user explicitly asks in that message.** Implementing a pass is not permission to commit. Wait until they have reviewed and said to commit.

- Never `git commit` “because the pass is done.”
- Never `--no-verify`, `--no-gpg-sign`, force-push to `main`/`master`, or `reset --hard` unless they request that exact action.
- Never `git add .` blindly. Do not stage `.env`, credentials, or `docs/brief.pdf`.
- Do not update git config. Do not use interactive git (`-i`).
- Do not push or open a PR unless they ask.

When they **do** ask to commit:

- One TODO pass per commit. Do not squash unrelated passes.
- Follow the repo protocol: status, diff, log; HEREDOC message; no secrets.
- [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/): `type(scope): short summary` (e.g. `feat(capability): add filesystem registry`).
- Title is brief. Then **two blank lines**. Then a body that explains why.
- Types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `test`, `refactor`, `chore` as appropriate.

```text
feat(capability): persist tenant overrides on disk


Header-only overrides enroll a tenant without duplicating the base artifact.
```

If asked to “finish the pass,” stop at working tree changes and say the diff is ready for review.
